  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 154 AM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nMonitoring the potential for some early morning rain showers this \nmorning. Area of focus is mainly over central South Dakota, although \nthere is some signal in model guidance for some rain over parts of \nnortheastern South Dakota and western Minnesota as well. There is a \nfair degree of model consensus for these showers to develop and move \noverhead beginning around 08-09Z and continuing through sunrise and \ninto the morning hours. Expecting only light rain out of these \nshowers, with little accumulation. \n\nMore robust chances for rain arrive Sunday night into Monday \nmorning. Ensemble medians for precipitation continue to increase, \nand models have trended the heaviest precipitation further south as \nwell. The heaviest rainfall is now expected over parts of eastern \nSouth Dakota, with medians up over an inch in that area. NBM \nprobability to see an inch or more for the 24 hours ending Monday \nevening ranges from around 40 to 60 percent along the US-212 \ncorridor. While no severe weather is expected with this rain, there \nis some hint to some flooding potential due to heavy rain. The main \ndriver of this signal is a plume of strong moisture into the area, \nand PWATs sit at around 1.5\"-1.75\" Monday morning. This would be \nwell over 90th percentile values for this time of year by about \n0.25\"-0.5\". Given the warmth above the surface, the warm cloud layer \nis expected to be somewhere between 11000-12000 feet, another \ningredient in the flooding formula. With forcing coming from a 50+ \nknot low-level jet, storm motion will likely be fairly quick, \nmeaning that a training storm setup will be necessary for flooding \nto develop. Perhaps the greatest limitation is a lack of instability \nin the area. MUCAPE values struggle to hit even 500 J/kg, so there \nis much to be desired. All this to say that while flooding can't \nquite be ruled out, it does appear to be somewhat of an edge case \nwith otherwise benign rainfall. Latest Flash Flood Guidance values \nstill exceed NBM 90th percentile values, so in all likelihood \nwhatever rain falls, the ground will be able to handle. It should \nalso be noted that while there is currently a focus on the US-212 \ncorridor, the recent shift southward should be a reminder that \nfurther shifts of the axis of heaviest rainfall will still be \npossible despite a limited time frame before the precipitation \narrives. \n\nBehind the rain Monday morning, a cold front will pass through the \nforecast area Monday afternoon. As the front passes, winds will turn \nfrom southerly out ahead to northwesterly behind. These \nnorthwesterly winds will increase to 20 to 30 miles per hour, \nparticularly along and west of the Missouri River. Gusts may exceed \nWind Advisory criteria at times, although the latest NBM guidance \nkeeps those chances (~20-50%, increasing moving northwest) over \nCorson and Dewey Counties. While no product will be issued on this \nshift, a headline may be needed with future forecast packages, \nespecially with the NBM's tendency to under-forecast wind \nspeeds under a cold air advection regime. \n\nTemperatures Tuesday will return to slightly below normal (highs in \nthe mid to upper 60s) after a brief warmup to near-normal on Monday. \nMid-week, a weak upper-level ridge begins to build overhead. This \nwill lead to a gradual warming trend through the end of the week. \nExpect multiple shortwaves to make their way into the region and \nbring additional chances for rain to the forecast area.",
